1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,408
Welcome to chapter four of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,408 --> 00:00:09,886
Attention is the star, but a star needs a stage.

3
00:00:09,886 --> 00:00:25,391
Today we assemble the complete transformer: positional encodings, feed forward networks, residual connections and layer normalisation, stacked into the architecture behind GPT, BERT and every modern language model.

4
00:00:25,391 --> 00:00:36,766
And as usual, I'll check everything against a real model, GPT-2 small, by counting its parameters, looking at its position vectors, and asking it a question.

5
00:00:37,616 --> 00:00:42,274
We have embeddings from chapter two and attention from chapter three.

6
00:00:42,274 --> 00:00:46,864
Today we build the whole transformer layer around them and stack it.

7
00:00:46,864 --> 00:00:50,171
This is the end of part one, the conceptual part.

8
00:00:50,171 --> 00:00:55,032
From chapter five, we start writing real code with the book's notebooks.

9
00:00:55,032 --> 00:00:59,824
As before, the snippets and GPT-2 experiments in this video are my own.

10
00:01:00,674 --> 00:01:01,695
Here's the plan.

11
00:01:01,695 --> 00:01:03,928
First, the missing piece: position.

12
00:01:03,928 --> 00:01:08,267
Then feed forward networks and why they need an activation function.

13
00:01:08,267 --> 00:01:13,817
Then residual connections, the highway system, and layer normalisation, the thermostat.

14
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:16,944
Then we assemble one complete layer and stack it.

15
00:01:16,944 --> 00:01:20,197
Then encoders versus decoders, and GPT versus BERT.

16
00:01:20,197 --> 00:01:23,898
Then training and inference, with real numbers from GPT-2.

17
00:01:23,898 --> 00:01:28,109
And we finish with exercises, the checkpoint and review questions.

18
00:01:28,959 --> 00:01:30,040
The vocabulary.

19
00:01:30,040 --> 00:01:36,530
A position encoding is a set of numbers added to an embedding to say where the token sits.

20
00:01:36,530 --> 00:01:46,409
A feed forward network is a small neural network applied to each position separately: expand by four, apply an activation, contract back.

21
00:01:46,409 --> 00:01:52,971
An activation function is the nonlinearity, like GELU or ReLU, that makes depth worthwhile.

22
00:01:52,971 --> 00:01:57,081
A residual connection adds a layer's input to its output.

23
00:01:57,081 --> 00:02:01,191
LayerNorm and RMSNorm keep each vector in a stable range.

24
00:02:01,191 --> 00:02:11,214
An encoder lets every position see every other one, a decoder only lets positions look backward, and causal masking is how we enforce that.

25
00:02:12,064 --> 00:02:12,625
Part one.

26
00:02:12,625 --> 00:02:14,368
The missing piece, position.

27
00:02:15,218 --> 00:02:18,426
Here's the problem we skipped in chapter three.

28
00:02:18,426 --> 00:02:22,452
Attention compares every word with every word, all at once.

29
00:02:22,452 --> 00:02:25,113
There's no first word and no last word.

30
00:02:25,113 --> 00:02:30,095
So the dog chased the cat and the cat chased the dog look the same to it.

31
00:02:30,095 --> 00:02:40,194
I checked this in code: if you shuffle the input vectors, the attention outputs come out shuffled in exactly the same way, and nothing else changes.

32
00:02:40,194 --> 00:02:43,538
Mathematicians call that permutation equivariant.

33
00:02:43,538 --> 00:02:48,110
RNNs got order for free, because they read word one, then word two.

34
00:02:48,110 --> 00:02:54,252
We gave that up to gain parallelism, so we need another way to tell the model about order.

35
00:02:55,102 --> 00:02:56,301
The fix is simple.

36
00:02:56,301 --> 00:03:01,029
Give every position its own vector, and add it to the word's embedding.

37
00:03:01,029 --> 00:03:05,958
Cat at position one becomes the embedding of cat plus position vector one.

38
00:03:05,958 --> 00:03:11,552
Cat at position five gets position vector five instead, so the two end up different.

39
00:03:11,552 --> 00:03:14,816
The embedding says what, the position says where.

40
00:03:14,816 --> 00:03:19,344
It's added once, at the input, and then carried through every layer.

41
00:03:19,344 --> 00:03:29,933
The book compares it to GPS coordinates on a message from a search party: the coordinates don't change what the message says, they tell you where it came from.

42
00:03:30,783 --> 00:03:34,202
The original transformer used sinusoidal encodings.

43
00:03:34,202 --> 00:03:39,229
Each pair of dimensions is a sine and cosine wave at a different frequency.

44
00:03:39,229 --> 00:03:45,463
On the left you can see the fast waves in the low dimensions and slow waves in the high ones.

45
00:03:45,463 --> 00:03:57,127
The book uses a clock analogy: the hour and minute hands turn at different rates, and together they give every time a unique reading, while times a minute apart look similar.

46
00:03:57,127 --> 00:04:07,919
Sinusoidal encodings work the same way with many hands, and because they come from a formula, they're defined for any position, even ones never seen in training.

47
00:04:08,769 --> 00:04:13,952
Many models, including GPT-2, simply learn a position table instead.

48
00:04:13,952 --> 00:04:21,497
In GPT-2 that table is called wpe, and it's one thousand and twenty four rows by seven sixty eight.

49
00:04:21,497 --> 00:04:28,433
So GPT-2 can't handle position one thousand and twenty five, because there's no row for it.

50
00:04:28,433 --> 00:04:31,481
That's the trade-off the book describes.

51
00:04:31,481 --> 00:04:35,673
I compared the similarity of positions in both schemes.

52
00:04:35,673 --> 00:04:37,502
On the left, sinusoidal.

53
00:04:37,502 --> 00:04:40,246
On the right, GPT-2's learned table.

54
00:04:40,246 --> 00:04:54,651
GPT-2 discovered the same smoothness on its own: position ten and position eleven have a cosine of point nine nine eight, while position ten and position ninety are only at point two seven.

55
00:04:55,501 --> 00:04:59,703
Most recent models use rotary position embeddings, RoPE.

56
00:04:59,703 --> 00:05:08,183
Instead of adding a position vector, RoPE rotates the query and key vectors by an angle that depends on position.

57
00:05:08,183 --> 00:05:19,214
When you take the dot product of two rotated vectors, what's left depends on how far apart they are, so the model naturally sees relative distance.

58
00:05:19,214 --> 00:05:25,442
It's a fixed formula with no learned table, and it behaves better on long contexts.

59
00:05:25,442 --> 00:05:29,194
Llama, Mistral and most recent open models use it.

60
00:05:29,194 --> 00:05:39,250
One small correction: the book lists GPT-4 as using RoPE too, but OpenAI hasn't published GPT-4's architecture, so we can't know that.

61
00:05:40,099 --> 00:05:40,750
Part two.

62
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:42,339
Feed forward networks.

63
00:05:43,189 --> 00:05:45,860
Why do we need anything after attention?

64
00:05:45,860 --> 00:05:49,465
Because attention is fundamentally a mixing operation.

65
00:05:49,465 --> 00:05:56,208
It takes weighted averages of value vectors, so it gathers the ingredients but doesn't cook the meal.

66
00:05:56,208 --> 00:06:01,483
The feed forward network does the cooking, transforming each position's vector.

67
00:06:01,483 --> 00:06:05,889
Every transformer layer follows this rhythm: attend, then process.

68
00:06:05,889 --> 00:06:17,573
The book compares it to a factory: attention is the mail room that sorts related letters together, and the feed forward network is the station that actually reads each letter.

69
00:06:18,423 --> 00:06:21,337
The feed forward network has three steps.

70
00:06:21,337 --> 00:06:27,804
Expand the seven sixty eight numbers to three thousand and seventy two, four times as many.

71
00:06:27,804 --> 00:06:30,718
Apply a nonlinear activation to each one.

72
00:06:30,718 --> 00:06:36,048
Then contract back to seven sixty eight, so the output fits the next layer.

73
00:06:36,048 --> 00:06:45,856
I printed the shapes from real GPT-2: the expansion matrix is seven sixty eight by three oh seven two, and the contraction is the reverse.

74
00:06:45,856 --> 00:06:49,978
Crucially, every position goes through this independently.

75
00:06:49,978 --> 00:06:52,608
Cat, sat and the don't interact here.

76
00:06:52,608 --> 00:06:54,385
That was attention's job.

77
00:06:55,235 --> 00:06:57,017
Why expand then contract?

78
00:06:57,017 --> 00:06:59,870
Expansion gives the model scratch space.

79
00:06:59,870 --> 00:07:02,864
The book's analogy is long multiplication.

80
00:07:02,864 --> 00:07:14,273
Twenty three times forty seven: twenty three times forty is nine twenty, twenty three times seven is one sixty one, add them to get one thousand and eighty one.

81
00:07:14,273 --> 00:07:19,479
The intermediate numbers don't appear in the answer, but you needed them.

82
00:07:19,479 --> 00:07:25,112
The wide hidden layer gives the model room for intermediate features like that.

83
00:07:25,112 --> 00:07:32,242
And as we'll see, the feed forward layers hold about two thirds of GPT-2's non embedding parameters.

84
00:07:33,092 --> 00:07:36,812
Why do we need the activation function in the middle?

85
00:07:36,812 --> 00:07:40,954
Because without it, everything is linear: multiply and add.

86
00:07:40,954 --> 00:07:46,148
And a stack of linear operations collapses into a single linear operation.

87
00:07:46,148 --> 00:07:50,500
I checked it: B times A times v equals the matrix B A times v.

88
00:07:50,500 --> 00:07:54,641
So a hundred linear layers are no more expressive than one.

89
00:07:54,641 --> 00:08:01,169
The book's image is photocopying a photocopy: you'll never get a colour photograph out of it.

90
00:08:01,169 --> 00:08:04,327
Language is full of thresholds and reversals.

91
00:08:04,327 --> 00:08:08,469
Great job can be sincere or sarcastic depending on context.

92
00:08:08,469 --> 00:08:10,995
Capturing that needs a nonlinearity.

93
00:08:11,845 --> 00:08:14,795
Here are the two most common activations.

94
00:08:14,795 --> 00:08:19,328
ReLU passes positive values unchanged and zeroes the negatives.

95
00:08:19,328 --> 00:08:25,372
Its gradient there is zero, so units stuck in the negative region can stop learning.

96
00:08:25,372 --> 00:08:27,026
GELU is a smooth curve.

97
00:08:27,026 --> 00:08:35,732
It's close to the identity for large positive inputs and close to zero for large negatives, with a gentle dip in between.

98
00:08:35,732 --> 00:08:48,323
I computed the book's examples: GELU of two is one point nine five four, GELU of minus two is minus point zero four six, and GELU of minus a half is minus point one five four.

99
00:08:48,323 --> 00:08:49,762
They match the book.

100
00:08:49,762 --> 00:08:56,597
Think of thirty seventy two little judges, each deciding how much of its signal to let through.

101
00:08:57,447 --> 00:08:58,702
One correction.

102
00:08:58,702 --> 00:09:06,151
The book says most modern LLMs, naming GPT, Claude and Llama, use GELU or close variants.

103
00:09:06,151 --> 00:09:08,494
GPT-2 and GPT-3 do use GELU.

104
00:09:08,494 --> 00:09:18,704
But Llama uses SwiGLU, a gated feed forward block built on the SiLU activation, and Claude's architecture isn't published.

105
00:09:18,704 --> 00:09:30,337
The book's broader point stands: modern models use smooth nonlinear activations, and many now use gated variants of the feed forward block.

106
00:09:31,187 --> 00:09:31,949
Part three.

107
00:09:31,949 --> 00:09:33,405
Residual connections.

108
00:09:34,255 --> 00:09:36,738
Stacking many layers creates a problem.

109
00:09:36,738 --> 00:09:40,174
Imagine a game of telephone through ninety six layers.

110
00:09:40,174 --> 00:09:50,611
Even if each layer keeps ninety nine percent of the signal, point nine nine to the power ninety six is point three eight one, so only thirty eight percent survives.

111
00:09:50,611 --> 00:09:52,457
The book's number is correct.

112
00:09:52,457 --> 00:10:00,794
The same thing happens to gradients flowing backward during training: they can vanish or explode, so the early layers barely learn.

113
00:10:01,644 --> 00:10:03,706
The fix is beautifully simple.

114
00:10:03,706 --> 00:10:07,006
Add the input back to the output: x plus f of x.

115
00:10:07,006 --> 00:10:14,773
Now the layer only has to learn the residual, the change to make, rather than rebuilding everything from scratch.

116
00:10:14,773 --> 00:10:21,028
If a layer has nothing useful to add, it can learn f close to zero and just pass x through.

117
00:10:21,028 --> 00:10:24,328
The book calls it a highway with optional exits.

118
00:10:24,328 --> 00:10:31,958
Information can cruise straight from early layers to late ones, and take an exit only where a layer adds value.

119
00:10:31,958 --> 00:10:36,220
And during training, gradients get the same highway back down.

120
00:10:37,069 --> 00:10:45,279
In a transformer layer there are two skip connections, one around the attention block and one around the feed forward block.

121
00:10:45,279 --> 00:10:50,113
People call the vector that flows along this highway the residual stream.

122
00:10:50,113 --> 00:10:55,740
Each sublayer reads from the stream, computes something, and adds its update back in.

123
00:10:55,740 --> 00:11:03,288
That's also why every sublayer has to output exactly the same size as its input, seven sixty eight in GPT-2 small.

124
00:11:04,138 --> 00:11:04,892
Part four.

125
00:11:04,892 --> 00:11:06,399
Layer normalisation.

126
00:11:07,249 --> 00:11:10,950
Deep networks have a second problem: values can drift.

127
00:11:10,950 --> 00:11:23,697
If each layer amplifies its input by just one percent, after ninety six layers it's grown by two point six times, which again matches the book, and it can get much worse during training.

128
00:11:23,697 --> 00:11:26,575
LayerNorm resets the scale at every layer.

129
00:11:26,575 --> 00:11:41,172
For each vector, compute its mean and standard deviation across the dimensions, subtract the mean, divide by the standard deviation, and then apply a learned scale and shift so the network can undo it if it wants.

130
00:11:41,172 --> 00:11:43,297
The book calls it a thermostat.

131
00:11:44,147 --> 00:11:46,921
Let's check the book's LayerNorm example.

132
00:11:46,921 --> 00:11:54,499
The input is one oh two point five, ninety eight point three, one oh five point seven and ninety five point two.

133
00:11:54,499 --> 00:12:02,010
The book gives the output as point five two, minus point three one, one point one two, minus point seven eight.

134
00:12:02,010 --> 00:12:07,423
But those add up to point five five, and a normalised vector has to sum to zero.

135
00:12:07,423 --> 00:12:18,520
I ran PyTorch's layer norm with no scale or shift, and the correct output is point five two, minus point five three, one point three two, minus one point three one.

136
00:12:18,520 --> 00:12:25,489
The mean is one hundred point four two five and the standard deviation is about three point nine eight.

137
00:12:25,489 --> 00:12:27,722
The first value happens to match.

138
00:12:27,722 --> 00:12:34,286
A quick sanity check for any normalised vector: it should sum to zero and have a variance of one.

139
00:12:34,286 --> 00:12:36,045
This one is in the errata.

140
00:12:36,895 --> 00:12:38,605
Where does LayerNorm go?

141
00:12:38,605 --> 00:12:43,593
The original 2017 transformer normalised after each residual addition.

142
00:12:43,593 --> 00:12:45,304
That's called post norm.

143
00:12:45,304 --> 00:12:54,283
GPT-2 and most modern models normalise the input to each sublayer instead, before attention and before the feed forward block.

144
00:12:54,283 --> 00:13:03,689
That's pre norm, and it trains more stably, especially in very deep stacks, because the residual highway itself is never normalised.

145
00:13:04,539 --> 00:13:08,900
Many recent models, including Llama and Mistral, use RMSNorm instead.

146
00:13:08,900 --> 00:13:16,168
It skips the mean subtraction and just divides by the root mean square of the vector, then applies a learned scale.

147
00:13:16,168 --> 00:13:20,971
It's slightly faster, uses less memory, and works about as well in practice.

148
00:13:20,971 --> 00:13:28,365
On the same input it gives one point oh two, point nine seven eight, one point oh five two and point nine four seven.

149
00:13:28,365 --> 00:13:30,135
Notice those aren't centred.

150
00:13:30,135 --> 00:13:33,105
They're just rescaled to a typical size of one.

151
00:13:33,955 --> 00:13:34,607
Part five.

152
00:13:34,607 --> 00:13:36,173
Putting it all together.

153
00:13:37,024 --> 00:13:42,212
Here's one complete transformer layer, in the pre norm style that GPT-2 uses.

154
00:13:42,212 --> 00:13:43,762
Start with the input x.

155
00:13:43,762 --> 00:13:48,412
Normalise it, run multi head attention, and add the result back to x.

156
00:13:48,412 --> 00:13:50,164
That's the first residual.

157
00:13:50,164 --> 00:13:54,881
Then normalise again, run the feed forward network, and add that back.

158
00:13:54,881 --> 00:13:56,700
That's the second residual.

159
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:02,496
The output has the same shape as the input, so it can go straight into the next layer.

160
00:14:03,346 --> 00:14:14,333
In two lines of pseudocode: h equals x plus attention of layer norm of x, then the output equals h plus the feed forward network of layer norm of h.

161
00:14:14,333 --> 00:14:18,713
Six steps: normalise, attend, add, normalise, process, add.

162
00:14:18,713 --> 00:14:20,569
Then you stack the layer.

163
00:14:20,569 --> 00:14:29,330
GPT-2 small has twelve layers, GPT-3 has ninety six, and big models today have eighty to a hundred and twenty or more.

164
00:14:30,179 --> 00:14:32,781
The book compares the stack to a building.

165
00:14:32,781 --> 00:14:39,842
Every floor has the same layout, an attention room and a processing room, and the residual stream is the elevator.

166
00:14:39,842 --> 00:14:52,415
Researchers generally find that early layers capture local patterns and syntax, middle layers build phrases and semantic relationships, and late layers handle abstract structure and the final prediction.

167
00:14:52,415 --> 00:14:58,361
By the top floor, a simple word like sat carries who sat, where, and what's likely to come next.

168
00:14:59,211 --> 00:15:01,092
And here's the whole model.

169
00:15:01,092 --> 00:15:06,595
Token IDs go into the token embedding table, and position embeddings are added.

170
00:15:06,595 --> 00:15:09,799
The result flows through N transformer layers.

171
00:15:09,799 --> 00:15:20,108
A final layer norm, then a linear layer maps each vector back onto the vocabulary, producing a score, called a logit, for every possible next token.

172
00:15:20,108 --> 00:15:22,825
Softmax turns those into probabilities.

173
00:15:22,825 --> 00:15:24,427
That's GPT, end to end.

174
00:15:25,277 --> 00:15:28,667
I counted every parameter in the real GPT-2 small.

175
00:15:28,667 --> 00:15:36,192
The total is a hundred and twenty four million, four hundred and thirty nine thousand, eight hundred and eight.

176
00:15:36,192 --> 00:15:45,954
The token embedding table, fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven tokens by seven sixty eight, is thirty eight point six million on its own.

177
00:15:45,954 --> 00:15:55,648
Each layer has two point three six million parameters in attention and four point seven two million in the feed forward network, twice as many.

178
00:15:55,648 --> 00:16:03,512
Across twelve layers, that's fifty seven million in the feed forward networks and twenty eight million in attention.

179
00:16:03,512 --> 00:16:04,936
Layer norms are tiny.

180
00:16:04,936 --> 00:16:13,207
GPT-2 also reuses the token embedding matrix as its output layer, which is called weight tying, so it isn't counted twice.

181
00:16:14,057 --> 00:16:14,836
Part six.

182
00:16:14,836 --> 00:16:17,001
Encoders versus decoders.

183
00:16:17,851 --> 00:16:24,325
An encoder lets every position attend to every other position, so it has full, bidirectional visibility.

184
00:16:24,325 --> 00:16:30,550
A decoder uses a causal mask: each position can see itself and earlier positions, but nothing later.

185
00:16:30,550 --> 00:16:36,214
In the grid, the ticks below the diagonal are allowed and the crosses above it are blocked.

186
00:16:36,214 --> 00:16:40,696
When you're generating, you can't look at words you haven't written yet.

187
00:16:40,696 --> 00:16:42,128
That would be cheating.

188
00:16:42,978 --> 00:16:44,824
That gives three families.

189
00:16:44,824 --> 00:16:56,394
Encoder only models, like BERT, see everything, which makes them great for classification, entity extraction and retrieval, but they don't naturally generate text.

190
00:16:56,394 --> 00:17:03,350
Decoder only models, like GPT, Llama and Claude, are causal and generate text one token at a time.

191
00:17:03,350 --> 00:17:14,353
Encoder decoder models, like the original transformer and T5, read the source fully with an encoder, then generate with a decoder that cross attends to it.

192
00:17:14,353 --> 00:17:17,831
That's natural for translation and summarisation.

193
00:17:18,681 --> 00:17:22,972
The two famous lineages are BERT and GPT, both from 2018.

194
00:17:22,972 --> 00:17:27,790
BERT is encoder only and bidirectional, built for understanding.

195
00:17:27,790 --> 00:17:31,780
GPT is decoder only and causal, built for generation.

196
00:17:31,780 --> 00:17:42,622
As models scaled up, the decoders turned out to be good at understanding too, just by predicting what comes next, and they won the scaling race.

197
00:17:42,622 --> 00:17:46,160
ChatGPT, Claude and Llama are all decoder only.

198
00:17:46,160 --> 00:17:50,000
That's what we'll build in part three of this book.

199
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:59,485
And GPT stands for generative pre-trained transformer: it generates, it's pre-trained on lots of text, and it's a transformer.

200
00:18:00,335 --> 00:18:01,098
Part seven.

201
00:18:01,098 --> 00:18:03,386
From architecture to application.

202
00:18:04,236 --> 00:18:08,764
Training and inference use the same architecture in different ways.

203
00:18:08,764 --> 00:18:25,863
In training, we feed a sequence and ask the model to predict the next token at every position at once, measure how wrong it was with a loss, run the backward pass, and update every weight: embeddings, attention, feed forward networks, norms, everything.

204
00:18:25,863 --> 00:18:27,080
Billions of times.

205
00:18:27,080 --> 00:18:34,920
In inference, we feed a prompt, read the probabilities at the last position, pick a token, append it, and run again.

206
00:18:34,920 --> 00:18:39,515
One forward pass per new token, which is why long outputs take time.

207
00:18:40,365 --> 00:18:46,443
The book's inference example uses the capital of France is, with the model picking Paris.

208
00:18:46,443 --> 00:18:48,356
So I asked real GPT-2 small.

209
00:18:48,356 --> 00:18:55,594
Its top choice is not Paris but the word the, at eight and a half percent, followed by now, a, and France.

210
00:18:55,594 --> 00:18:58,872
Paris is only fifth, at three point two percent.

211
00:18:58,872 --> 00:19:05,701
If we always pick the most likely token, it continues the capital of the French Republic, and so on.

212
00:19:05,701 --> 00:19:07,955
That's a nice lesson about scale.

213
00:19:07,955 --> 00:19:17,926
A hundred and twenty four million parameter model, trained only to predict web text, knows Paris is relevant but doesn't answer like an assistant.

214
00:19:17,926 --> 00:19:23,389
Bigger models and instruction tuning, which come later in the book, change that.

215
00:19:24,239 --> 00:19:26,955
Here's how the same architecture scales.

216
00:19:26,955 --> 00:19:34,560
GPT-2 small: twelve layers, width seven sixty eight, twelve heads, a hundred and twenty four million parameters.

217
00:19:34,560 --> 00:19:41,350
GPT-2 large: thirty six layers, width twelve eighty, twenty heads, about seven seventy four million.

218
00:19:41,350 --> 00:19:50,245
GPT-3: ninety six layers, width twelve thousand two hundred and eighty eight, ninety six heads, a hundred and seventy five billion.

219
00:19:50,245 --> 00:20:00,973
Llama three point one four oh five B: a hundred and twenty six layers, width sixteen thousand three hundred and eighty four, a hundred and twenty eight heads.

220
00:20:00,973 --> 00:20:05,115
These figures come from the published papers and model cards.

221
00:20:05,115 --> 00:20:12,041
The design is the same; the scale changes, and with scale come abilities nobody fully understands yet.

222
00:20:12,891 --> 00:20:14,084
Let's take stock.

223
00:20:14,084 --> 00:20:17,383
You now know every major part of a transformer.

224
00:20:17,383 --> 00:20:19,839
Embeddings turn words into vectors.

225
00:20:19,839 --> 00:20:22,085
Position encodings add location.

226
00:20:22,085 --> 00:20:23,910
Attention gathers context.

227
00:20:23,910 --> 00:20:27,138
Feed forward networks transform each position.

228
00:20:27,138 --> 00:20:30,016
Residual connections preserve the signal.

229
00:20:30,016 --> 00:20:32,472
Layer norm keeps the values stable.

230
00:20:32,472 --> 00:20:34,297
And stacking builds depth.

231
00:20:34,297 --> 00:20:39,280
Every response from a modern chatbot flows through exactly this design.

232
00:20:40,130 --> 00:20:40,998
Part eight.

233
00:20:40,998 --> 00:20:41,709
Hands on.

234
00:20:42,559 --> 00:20:47,417
Exercises one and two ask you to trace the cat sat through the model.

235
00:20:47,417 --> 00:20:55,584
Tokenise it: in GPT-2 that's three tokens, with IDs four six four, three seven nine seven and three three three two.

236
00:20:55,584 --> 00:21:00,020
Look up embeddings, giving a three by seven sixty eight matrix.

237
00:21:00,020 --> 00:21:02,766
Add position vectors zero, one and two.

238
00:21:02,766 --> 00:21:07,554
In layer one's attention, sat queries for its subject and finds cat.

239
00:21:07,554 --> 00:21:11,496
The feed forward network transforms each row on its own.

240
00:21:11,496 --> 00:21:20,227
More layers refine the picture, and at the top we get a distribution over the next token, with words like on or down likely.

241
00:21:20,227 --> 00:21:25,859
And through it all, the residual stream keeps the original signal for cat alive.

242
00:21:26,709 --> 00:21:32,585
Exercise three: design a tiny transformer for predicting the last letter of a word.

243
00:21:32,585 --> 00:21:42,496
My choice would be two layers, width sixty four, four heads, and learned positions, because words are short and have a fixed maximum length.

244
00:21:42,496 --> 00:21:46,247
Exercise four asks which architecture fits each task.

245
00:21:46,247 --> 00:21:48,867
Sentiment classification: an encoder.

246
00:21:48,867 --> 00:21:54,530
Translation: classically an encoder decoder, though big decoders do it well too.

247
00:21:54,530 --> 00:21:56,653
Continuing a story: a decoder.

248
00:21:56,653 --> 00:21:58,919
Extracting entities: an encoder.

249
00:21:58,919 --> 00:22:07,130
Question answering over a passage: an encoder if you're extracting a span, a decoder if you're generating an answer.

250
00:22:07,980 --> 00:22:08,764
Part nine.

251
00:22:08,764 --> 00:22:10,647
The checkpoint exercise.

252
00:22:11,497 --> 00:22:13,437
The checkpoint has four parts.

253
00:22:13,437 --> 00:22:21,846
First, draw one transformer layer from memory, with both layer norms, attention, the feed forward network and both residual paths.

254
00:22:21,846 --> 00:22:27,215
Second, pick a word from the quick brown fox jumps and trace it through two layers.

255
00:22:27,215 --> 00:22:29,544
Third, answer four design questions.

256
00:22:29,544 --> 00:22:36,077
And fourth, pick the architecture for a document question answerer, an essay writer and a translator.

257
00:22:36,077 --> 00:22:39,763
Pause the video and try it before you look at my answers.

258
00:22:40,613 --> 00:22:41,371
My answers.

259
00:22:41,371 --> 00:22:48,256
Without residuals, a deep stack degrades and the gradients vanish, so the early layers barely learn.

260
00:22:48,256 --> 00:22:54,522
Without layer norm, activations drift and blow up or shrink, and training becomes unstable.

261
00:22:54,522 --> 00:23:06,640
Positions have to be added before attention, because attention is blind to order: the position information needs to be inside the queries and keys when the scores are computed.

262
00:23:06,640 --> 00:23:15,660
More layers give more capacity and more refinement, but they cost compute, are harder to optimise, and can overfit small data sets.

263
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:25,023
And for part four: a document question answerer is naturally an encoder, an essay writer a decoder, and a translator an encoder decoder.

264
00:23:25,873 --> 00:23:26,568
Part ten.

265
00:23:26,568 --> 00:23:27,495
Wrapping up.

266
00:23:28,345 --> 00:23:29,722
The key takeaways.

267
00:23:29,722 --> 00:23:38,212
One: attention is blind to order, so we add position information, with sinusoidal, learned or rotary encodings.

268
00:23:38,212 --> 00:23:46,167
Two: the feed forward network expands by four, applies GELU, and contracts, separately at each position.

269
00:23:46,167 --> 00:23:51,827
Three: residual connections, x plus f of x, are the highway through depth.

270
00:23:51,827 --> 00:24:00,088
Four: LayerNorm or RMSNorm keeps values in a stable range, and modern models normalise before each sublayer.

271
00:24:00,088 --> 00:24:05,978
Five: a layer is norm, attend, add, norm, feed forward, add, stacked N times.

272
00:24:05,978 --> 00:24:11,944
Six: encoders are bidirectional, decoders are causal, and GPT is decoder only.

273
00:24:12,794 --> 00:24:15,645
My notes on the book for this chapter.

274
00:24:15,645 --> 00:24:22,023
The compounding numbers, the GELU values and the feed forward shapes are all correct.

275
00:24:22,023 --> 00:24:24,349
Three items go into the errata.

276
00:24:24,349 --> 00:24:36,054
The LayerNorm example output is wrong, and the correct values are point five two, minus point five three, one point three two and minus one point three one.

277
00:24:36,054 --> 00:24:41,907
Llama uses SwiGLU rather than GELU, and Claude's architecture isn't published.

278
00:24:41,907 --> 00:24:47,159
And GPT-4's architecture, including its position scheme, isn't public.

279
00:24:47,159 --> 00:24:53,087
Finally, the Paris example is illustrative: real GPT-2 small ranks Paris fifth.

280
00:24:53,937 --> 00:24:56,339
Review questions, with my answers.

281
00:24:56,339 --> 00:24:58,318
One: why position encodings?

282
00:24:58,318 --> 00:25:05,950
Because attention treats its inputs as an unordered set, so without them dog bites man equals man bites dog.

283
00:25:05,950 --> 00:25:17,538
Two: the two main approaches are sinusoidal, a formula that extrapolates, and learned, a table that often works better within range but stops at the maximum length.

284
00:25:17,538 --> 00:25:20,082
RoPE is the modern relative variant.

285
00:25:20,082 --> 00:25:30,682
Three: the feed forward network is a per position network that expands for scratch space, applies a nonlinearity, and contracts to fit the next layer.

286
00:25:30,682 --> 00:25:33,579
Four: residuals are a highway with exits.

287
00:25:33,579 --> 00:25:38,667
Adding x back preserves both the signal and the gradients through depth.

288
00:25:39,517 --> 00:25:49,472
Five: layer norm stops values drifting by normalising each vector to mean zero and variance one, then applying a learned scale and shift.

289
00:25:49,472 --> 00:25:52,669
Modern models apply it before each sublayer.

290
00:25:52,669 --> 00:25:59,573
Six: one layer is layer norm, multi head attention, add x, layer norm, feed forward, add again.

291
00:25:59,573 --> 00:26:04,587
Seven: encoders use full attention and suit understanding, like BERT.

292
00:26:04,587 --> 00:26:08,947
Decoders use causal attention and suit generation, like GPT.

293
00:26:08,947 --> 00:26:16,504
Eight: the causal mask sets future scores to minus infinity before the softmax, so they get zero weight.

294
00:26:16,504 --> 00:26:22,753
It's needed so that a model trained to predict the next token can't simply look at it.

295
00:26:23,603 --> 00:26:24,833
Your practice list.

296
00:26:24,833 --> 00:26:29,170
Draw the pre norm layer from memory and check it against the slide.

297
00:26:29,170 --> 00:26:31,760
Verify the LayerNorm correction by hand.

298
00:26:31,760 --> 00:26:35,644
Load GPT-2 and count its parameters with one line of Python.

299
00:26:35,644 --> 00:26:39,787
Do exercises three and four and the checkpoint design questions.

300
00:26:39,787 --> 00:26:45,483
And get your Python environment ready, because the book's chapter five notebook is next.

301
00:26:45,483 --> 00:26:50,014
The code in this video is my own, since part one has no book notebook.

302
00:26:50,864 --> 00:26:53,853
That's chapter four, and the end of part one.

303
00:26:53,853 --> 00:27:01,756
You understand what AI is, how words become numbers, how attention works, and how the transformer puts it all together.

304
00:27:01,756 --> 00:27:03,151
Part two is hands on.

305
00:27:03,151 --> 00:27:08,929
In chapter five we set up Python and start writing real code with the book's notebooks.

306
00:27:08,929 --> 00:27:10,457
The theory is in place.

307
00:27:10,457 --> 00:27:11,387
Time to build.

308
